      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "To compare the anti-inflammatory and analgesic effects of topical pranoprofen 0.1% with diclofenac sodium 0.1% after strabismus surgery, 40 patients were prospectively randomized and assigned into 2 groups. Signs and symptoms of inflammation, as well as intraocular pressure (IOP) of patients, were evaluated at 1 day and 1 and 3 weeks following surgery. Both groups reported reduced inflammation and discomfort at 1 week, as compared to 1 day. There was no statistically significant difference in any measurement of postoperative inflammation (i.e., discomfort, chemosis, secretion, conjunctival hyperemia, and conjunctival gap size) between the 2 groups at any time. None of the patients developed an allergic reaction to the medications and IOP measurements were within the normal limits in both groups. Pranoprofen 0.1% was found to be as effective as diclofenac sodium 0.1% in reducing inflammation and pain after strabismus surgery. Pranoprofen could be used as a safe and effective anti-inflammatory alternative for the treatment of inflammation following strabismus surgery."},
